#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724871 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\qquad y=2t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is given by $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2t$. Let $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=1$ into both parametric equations to find the point:\n\n$$\nx=2(1)+1=3,\\qquad y=2(1)^2+1=3.\n$$\n\nThus $C=(3,3)$. The supplied derivative gives the tangent slope at $C$:\n\n$$\nm=2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(3,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-3&=-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}+3\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724873 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\ln(t^2)+1,\\qquad y=2t^3-3t+3,\\qquad t>0.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C$. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal line is $y=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First find the coordinates of $C$ by substituting $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\ln(1^2)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=2(1)^3-3(1)+3=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate both coordinates with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{2t}{t^2}=\\frac{2}{t},\\qquad y'(t)=6t^2-3.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of these derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t^2-3}{2/t}=\\frac{t(6t^2-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=1$ this gives\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTake its negative reciprocal to obtain the normal slope:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(1,2)$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-2&=-\\frac{2}{3}(x-1),\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}+2,\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEnter $-\\dfrac{2}{3}x+\\dfrac{8}{3}$ in the blank after $y=$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724889 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos t+1,\\qquad y=2\\sin t-4,\\qquad 0<t<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "For a vertical tangent, we need $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t)\\ne0$. The coordinate derivatives are\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-3\\sin t,\\qquad y'(t)=2\\cos t.\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t)=0$ gives $\\sin t=0$, so the candidates on the closed interval $[0,2\\pi]$ are $0$, $\\pi$, and $2\\pi$. The problem specifies the open interval $0<t<2\\pi$, so only $t=\\pi$ remains.\n\nCheck the other derivative:\n\n$$\ny'(\\pi)=2\\cos\\pi=-2\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the tangent is vertical only at $t=\\pi$ on the specified interval. At $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, it is $y'(t)$ that is zero, while $x'(t)$ is nonzero; those tangents are horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724892 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3(t+2),\\qquad y=2(t-1)^2+4,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope of a tangent to a parametric curve is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal tangent, we need $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\qquad y'(t)=4(t-1).\n$$\n\nSolve for the zero of $y'(t)$:\n\n$$\n4(t-1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=1.\n$$\n\nSince $x'(1)=3\\ne0$, the tangent is horizontal at $t=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724896 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^2+2}{t+1},\\qquad y=t^2+2t,\\qquad -1<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A vertical tangent requires $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$. Using the quotient rule for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{2t(t+1)-(t^2+2)}{(t+1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2+2t-2}{(t+1)^2},\\\\\ny'(t)&=2t+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $t>-1$, the denominator $(t+1)^2$ is nonzero. Hence $x'(t)=0$ exactly when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^2+2t-2&=0\\\\\n(t+1)^2&=3\\\\\nt&=-1\\pm\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe root $-1-\\sqrt{3}$ is outside the given interval. The remaining candidate is $t=\\sqrt{3}-1$. Check that its other coordinate derivative is nonzero:\n\n$$\ny'(\\sqrt{3}-1)=2(\\sqrt{3}-1)+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\ne0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is vertical at $t=\\sqrt{3}-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724956 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^2+3}{t-1},\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -2<t<4,\\quad t\\ne1.\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is horizontal at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$ at parameters in the given domain.\n\nUsing the quotient rule for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{2t(t-1)-(t^2+3)}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2-2t-3}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(t-3)(t+1)}{(t-1)^2},\\\\\ny'(t)&=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $t\\ne1$, the denominator of $x'(t)$ is nonzero. Hence $x'(t)=0$ exactly when\n\n$$\n(t-3)(t+1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=-1\\ \\text{or}\\ t=3.\n$$\n\nBoth values lie in $-2<t<4$ and neither equals the excluded parameter $1$. The value $t=1$ is not a candidate: the original curve is undefined there.\n\nCheck the other derivative:\n\n$$\ny'(-1)=-2\\ne0,\\qquad y'(3)=6\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the normal is horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$, in that order. Although $y'(0)=0$, we have $x'(0)=-3\\ne0$, so $t=0$ gives a horizontal tangent and a vertical normal instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724964 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t,\\qquad y=t^3+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2t-6,\\qquad y'(t)=3t^2+1.\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t-6=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=3.\n$$\n\nAt this parameter,\n\n$$\ny'(3)=3(3)^2+1=28\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the tangent is vertical and the normal is horizontal at $t=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724969 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+2,\\\\\ny&=2\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-1,\\\\\n&\\hspace{1em}0\\le t<2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical when the tangent is horizontal. We therefore need $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=-3\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right),\\\\\ny'(t)&=2\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $y'(t)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=0,\\\\\nt-\\frac{\\pi}{6}&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi,\\\\\nt&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}+k\\pi,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $k=0$ and $k=1$ place $t$ in $0\\le t<2\\pi$, giving $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$. Check the other derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=-3\\ne0,\\\\\nx'\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)=3\\ne0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$, in that order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725116 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x,y)=(x^2+y)^2$ at the point $A(1,-2)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(A)=\\nabla g(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nThe direction is from $A$ towards $B$, so subtract the coordinates in that order and normalize:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -2-1,2-(-2)\\rangle=\\langle -3,4\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2}=5,\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\left\\langle -\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule to both partial derivatives, then evaluate at $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(x,y)&=2(x^2+y)(2x)=4x(x^2+y),\\\\\ng_y(x,y)&=2(x^2+y),\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,-2)&=\\langle 4(1)(1-2),2(1-2)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -4,-2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(1,-2)&=\\langle -4,-2\\rangle\\cdot\\left\\langle -\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\frac{(-4)(-3)+(-2)(4)}{5}\\\\\n&=\\frac{12-8}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725117 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x^2+2xy$ at the point $(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=2\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nFirst, normalize the supplied direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{5},\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\langle 2,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives and evaluate the gradient at $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x+2y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=2x,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 2(1)+2(2),2(1)\\rangle=\\langle 6,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(1,2)&=\\langle 6,2\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle 2,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{5}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{6(2)+2(-1)}{\\sqrt{5}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725118 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $h(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y}{x-y}$ at the point $A(2,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-1,3)$.\n\nThe required directional derivative is {{field-1}}. Give an exact value." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}h(A)=\\nabla h(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nFirst find the direction from $A$ towards $B$ and its unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -1-2,3-1\\rangle=\\langle -3,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{13},\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\langle -3,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2-1=1\\ne 0$, the function is differentiable near $A$. Use the quotient rule for each partial derivative. In the $y$ derivative, the derivative of the denominator $x-y$ is $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_x(x,y)&=\\frac{2x(x-y)-(x^2+y)}{(x-y)^2},\\\\\nh_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(x-y)+(x^2+y)}{(x-y)^2},\\\\\nh_x(2,1)&=\\frac{4(1)-5}{1^2}=-1,\\\\\nh_y(2,1)&=\\frac{1+5}{1^2}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the gradient at the starting point $A$, then take the dot product with the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}h(2,1)&=\\langle -1,6\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle -3,2\\rangle}{\\sqrt{13}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(-1)(-3)+6(2)}{\\sqrt{13}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{13}}=\\frac{15\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725124 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\sqrt{t + 1} \\\\ y&=- t^{3} - 3 \\\\ t>-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right) + 1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(1\\right)^{3} - 3 \\\\ &=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t + 1}} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 3 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{-3}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}}=- 6 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(\\sqrt{2},-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+4&=- 6 \\sqrt{2}\\left(x-\\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ y&=- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 12+\\left(-4\\right) \\\\ y&=- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725126 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{5} - 3 t^{3} \\\\ y&=- 2 t^{2} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=2 \\left(-1\\right)^{5} - 3 \\left(-1\\right)^{3} \\\\ &=1 \\\\ y_0&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 9 t^{2} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 4 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(-1)}{x\\prime(-1)}&=\\frac{4}{1}=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+2&=4\\left(x-1\\right) \\\\ y&=4 x - 4+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ y&=4 x - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725127 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{5} - t^{3} \\\\ y&=- t^{2} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=2 \\left(1\\right)^{5} - \\left(1\\right)^{3} \\\\ &=1 \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 3 t^{2} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{-2}{7}=- \\frac{2}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+1&=- \\frac{2}{7}\\left(x-1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{2 x}{7} + \\frac{2}{7}+\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{2 x}{7} - \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725130 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t \\\\ y&=- t^{5} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=-4 \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(-1\\right)^{5} \\\\ &=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=- 4 t + 2 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 5 t^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(-1)}{x\\prime(-1)}&=\\frac{-5}{6}=- \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(-4,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=- \\frac{5}{6}\\left(x+4\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{10}{3}+\\left(1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725134 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\sqrt{t + 3} \\\\ y&=3 t^{3} + 4 \\\\ t>-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right) + 3} \\\\ &=2 \\\\ y_0&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{3} + 4 \\\\ &=7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t + 3}} \\\\ y'(t)&=9 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{9}{\\frac{1}{4}}=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(2,7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-7&=36\\left(x-2\\right) \\\\ y&=36 x - 72+\\left(7\\right) \\\\ y&=36 x - 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725149 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=xy^2-3\\ln(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point is in the domain because $x+z=3>0$. A differentiable function decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to its gradient.\n\nCompute all three partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=y^2-\\frac{3}{x+z},\\\\\ng_y&=2xy,\\\\\ng_z&=-\\frac{3}{x+z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm contributes to the $x$ and $z$ components, but not the $y$ component. At the given point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(1,2,2)&=\\left\\langle 2^2-\\frac{3}{1+2},\\,2(1)(2),\\,-\\frac{3}{1+2}\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,4,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReverse every component to obtain the direction of fastest decrease:\n\n$$\n-\\nabla g(1,2,2)=\\langle -3,-4,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nAny positive multiple of this vector represents the same direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725153 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x^2+xy+2y^2$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-1)$?" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable function increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x+y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x+4y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,-1)&=(2(1)+(-1))\\mathbf{i}+(1+4(-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&=\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of $\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}$. A direction vector need not have unit length; any positive multiple points in the same direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725158 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $h(x,y,z)=(x+y)e^{xz-y}+\\ln(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point is in the domain because $x+z=2>0$. The direction of fastest decrease is opposite to the gradient.\n\nDifferentiate before substituting the point. The product rule is needed for the $x$ and $y$ partial derivatives, and the exponential requires the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_x&=e^{xz-y}+(x+y)z e^{xz-y}+\\frac{1}{x+z},\\\\\nh_y&=e^{xz-y}-(x+y)e^{xz-y},\\\\\nh_z&=(x+y)x e^{xz-y}+\\frac{1}{x+z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ partial derivative, the minus sign comes from $\\partial(xz-y)/\\partial y=-1$. The logarithm contributes only to the $x$ and $z$ partial derivatives.\n\nAt $(1,1,1)$, the exponential is $e^{1(1)-1}=1$ and $x+y=x+z=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla h(1,1,1)&=\\left\\langle 1+2(1)+\\frac12,\\,1-2,\\,2(1)+\\frac12\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\left\\langle \\frac72,-1,\\frac52\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNegate all three components:\n\n$$\n-\\nabla h(1,1,1)=\\left\\langle -\\frac72,1,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $h$ decreases most rapidly in this direction. Multiplying this vector by a positive number does not change its direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725176 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x^2-3y^2$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle 4,-3\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}g(1,1)$, where $\\mathbf u$ is the unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst normalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{4^2+(-3)^2}=5,\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\left\\langle\\frac45,-\\frac35\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext compute the gradient and evaluate it at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x,y)&=\\langle 4x,-6y\\rangle,\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,1)&=\\langle 4,-6\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(1,1)&=\\nabla g(1,1)\\cdot\\mathbf u\\\\\n&=4\\left(\\frac45\\right)+(-6)\\left(-\\frac35\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{16+18}{5}=\\frac{34}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe positive sign means that $g$ increases in this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725180 :question/problem "At a point $P$, a differentiable function $f(x,y)$ has gradient $\\nabla f(P)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$.\n\nWhich expression gives the rate of change of $f$ at $P$ in the direction of $\\mathbf v=\\langle 3,4\\rangle$? You do not need to evaluate the expression." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in a direction is the directional derivative\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(P)=\\nabla f(P)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the specified direction. Since $\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}=\\left\\langle\\frac35,\\frac45\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus the required expression is\n\n$$\n\\langle 2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\left\\langle\\frac35,\\frac45\\right\\rangle\n=\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{5}.\n$$\n\nDividing by $5$ makes the rate a change per unit distance; using $\\mathbf v$ without normalizing would give a rate for a different speed of travel."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725181 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+e^{2x-y}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(1,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "First compute the gradient, using the chain rule for the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2y+2e^{2x-y},\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=2x-e^{2x-y},\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 4+2,2-1\\rangle=\\langle 6,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe direction of steepest decrease is opposite to the gradient. The corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=-\\frac{\\nabla f(1,2)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,2)\\|}\n=\\frac{\\langle-6,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{37}}.\n$$\n\nThe rate in that direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(1,2)&=\\langle6,1\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle-6,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{37}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{-36-1}{\\sqrt{37}}=-\\sqrt{37}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\|\\nabla f\\|\\cos\\theta$ is smallest when $\\theta=\\pi$. The magnitude of the decrease is $\\sqrt{37}$, but the signed rate of change is $-\\sqrt{37}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725197 :question/problem "![](images/84/84d563cdb67d07391e980881cf68aea58ffe98b678f045530aabae6adbbabc79.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3,240^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=240^\\circ-30^\\circ=210^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,210^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3\\cos\\left(210^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\y &=3\\sin\\left(210^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725209 :question/problem "![](images/81/812ca42ad6f3b14494f3a4118211e4b25a7930c41910f8613a22e03e52af5cd3.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(7,315^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $7$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=315^\\circ+15^\\circ=330^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(7,330^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=7\\cos\\left(330^\\circ\\right)=7\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\y &=7\\sin\\left(330^\\circ\\right)=7\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725219 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 t - 4 \\\\ y&=\\left(t + 3\\right)^{3} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A horizontal tangent requires $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2 \\\\ y'(t)&=3 \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $y'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}&=0 \\\\ t&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3)&=2\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is horizontal at $t=-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725230 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=3 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{5} - 2 \\\\ y&=t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A vertical tangent requires $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=15 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{4} \\\\ y'(t)&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $x'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{4}&=0 \\\\ t&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'(1)&=1\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is vertical at $t=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725232 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 t - 2 \\\\ y&=2 - 2 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A horizontal tangent requires $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 10 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $y'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 10 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{4}&=0 \\\\ t&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1)&=2\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is horizontal at $t=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725248 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 - 2 t \\\\ y&=1 - 2 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A horizontal tangent requires $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-2 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 4 t - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $y'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 t - 8&=0 \\\\ t&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-2)&=-2\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is horizontal at $t=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725250 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=t - 3 \\\\ y&=\\left(t + 6\\right)^{5} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A horizontal tangent requires $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=1 \\\\ y'(t)&=5 \\left(t + 6\\right)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $y'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 \\left(t + 6\\right)^{4}&=0 \\\\ t&=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-6)&=1\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is horizontal at $t=-6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725352 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral CDAB with measurements](images/1e/1e20144abb107eeb5c452cecfad79159103871bcdd9e96cf3ec60ff461ece80d.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ is $3\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $CD=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CB=7\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $66^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle DAB$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}^{2}$.\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $CDB$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDB}&=\\frac12\\cdot CD\\cdot CB\\cdot\\sin\\angle DCB\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 4\\cdot 7\\cdot\\sin66^\\circ\\\\&\\approx12.789636\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ and the missing triangle $DAB$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAB}&=A_{CDB}-A_{CDAB}\\\\&=14\\sin66^\\circ-3\\\\&\\approx9.789636\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $10\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725353 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral CDAB with measurements](images/fd/fdb4f6732092a0d7312a7df801cd3cace1196cfb705c9d9b38b868aff39bf34f.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ is $4\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $CD=3.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $CB=9\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $105^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle DAB$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $CDB$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDB}&=\\frac12\\cdot CD\\cdot CB\\cdot\\sin\\angle DCB\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 3.5\\cdot 9\\cdot\\sin105^\\circ\\\\&\\approx15.213332\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ and the missing triangle $DAB$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAB}&=A_{CDB}-A_{CDAB}\\\\&=15.75\\sin105^\\circ-4\\\\&\\approx11.213332\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $11\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725360 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral BCDA with measurements](images/c7/c715f2d6e3221f2061abe962c22fde1c08db8beccb1a5367cce1869aed7c1e3c.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ is $3\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $BC=7\\,\\text{in}$ and $BA=3.5\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $63^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle CDA$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{in}^{2}$.\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $BCA$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCA}&=\\frac12\\cdot BC\\cdot BA\\cdot\\sin\\angle CBA\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 7\\cdot 3.5\\cdot\\sin63^\\circ\\\\&\\approx10.914830\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ and the missing triangle $CDA$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDA}&=A_{BCA}-A_{BCDA}\\\\&=12.25\\sin63^\\circ-3\\\\&\\approx7.914830\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $8\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725361 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral BCDA with measurements](images/da/dae3b48863de88e3d9d55eee41974aaad296fb12a69e90340177a6c6230fd2b5.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ is $5\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $BC=6.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $BA=9\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $45^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle CDA$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $BCA$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCA}&=\\frac12\\cdot BC\\cdot BA\\cdot\\sin\\angle CBA\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 9\\cdot\\sin45^\\circ\\\\&\\approx20.682873\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ and the missing triangle $CDA$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDA}&=A_{BCA}-A_{BCDA}\\\\&=29.25\\sin45^\\circ-5\\\\&\\approx15.682873\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $16\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725362 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral DABC with measurements](images/e3/e3e3c729c3e4a2f58d36eea64437187ffee71588222a27675a4e3f5026499c40.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $DABC$ is $6\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $DA=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $DC=5\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $105^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle ABC$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}^{2}$.\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $DAC$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAC}&=\\frac12\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot\\sin\\angle ADC\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 4\\cdot 5\\cdot\\sin105^\\circ\\\\&\\approx9.659258\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $DABC$ and the missing triangle $ABC$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{ABC}&=A_{DAC}-A_{DABC}\\\\&=10\\sin105^\\circ-6\\\\&\\approx3.659258\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $4\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725377 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with measurements](images/04/0421bc771a9755ed2c02fc468ee050a8157259c7bdd6501a064ba5afec93e385.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $9\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $AB=9\\,\\text{in}$ and $AD=5\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $117^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{in}^{2}$.\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $ABD$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{ABD}&=\\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot\\sin\\angle BAD\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 9\\cdot 5\\cdot\\sin117^\\circ\\\\&\\approx20.047647\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and the missing triangle $BCD$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCD}&=A_{ABD}-A_{ABCD}\\\\&=22.5\\sin117^\\circ-9\\\\&\\approx11.047647\\,\\text{in}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $11\\,\\text{in}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725378 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral CDAB with measurements](images/00/0032bb8638ce6c2d7f84da559201d315d252d736119c937ffd16bbd509fcd050.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $CD=11\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CB=7\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $66^\\circ$. The area of $\\triangle DAB$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is {{field-1}} $\\text{cm}^{2}$.\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $CDB$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDB}&=\\frac12\\cdot CD\\cdot CB\\cdot\\sin\\angle DCB\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 11\\cdot 7\\cdot\\sin66^\\circ\\\\&\\approx35.171500\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ and the missing triangle $DAB$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAB}&=A_{CDB}-A_{CDAB}\\\\&=38.5\\sin66^\\circ-10\\\\&\\approx25.171500\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $25\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725379 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral CDAB with measurements](images/f5/f544bbf3271570f4a237e6dd773955be0c23ce53e4c7ca30dade6fbdf927cf7c.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ is $5\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $CD=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CB=10\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $105^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle DAB$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $CDB$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDB}&=\\frac12\\cdot CD\\cdot CB\\cdot\\sin\\angle DCB\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 4\\cdot 10\\cdot\\sin105^\\circ\\\\&\\approx19.318517\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $CDAB$ and the missing triangle $DAB$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{DAB}&=A_{CDB}-A_{CDAB}\\\\&=20\\sin105^\\circ-5\\\\&\\approx14.318517\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $14\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725385 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral BCDA with measurements](images/b7/b7b9085c5c5cbd4c1a466fa7e70bb83c6616169b5471f4925a445f0721734a8d.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ is $5\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$. The sides $BC=9\\,\\text{cm}$ and $BA=4\\,\\text{cm}$ meet at $42^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle CDA$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing triangle $BCA$. Use the two given sides and their included angle, with the calculator in degree mode.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{BCA}&=\\frac12\\cdot BC\\cdot BA\\cdot\\sin\\angle CBA\\\\&=\\frac12\\cdot 9\\cdot 4\\cdot\\sin42^\\circ\\\\&\\approx12.044351\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe enclosing triangle consists of the quadrilateral $BCDA$ and the missing triangle $CDA$. Subtract the quadrilateral area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A_{CDA}&=A_{BCA}-A_{BCDA}\\\\&=18\\sin42^\\circ-5\\\\&\\approx7.044351\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep full precision until this subtraction is complete. Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $7\\,\\text{cm}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725389 :question/problem "![Concave quadrilateral ABCD with measurements](images/24/24879e3d83941369eeda1d10ef9b82e08d4616af8d5696067eb8ed3748094cce.png)\n\nThe area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $6\\,\\text{in}^{2}$. The sides $AB=7\\,\\text{in}$ and $AD=9\\,\\text{in}$ meet at $45^\\circ$. What is the area of $\\triangle BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**?\n\nYou may use a scientific calculator." :question/worked-solution "First calculate the area of the enclosing tria